I was always listed there.

482
00:23:51,467 --> 00:23:52,633
Why did you pay five pesos?

483
00:23:52,633 --> 00:23:55,233
- Because you were on the list?
- Yes, you'd pay for that.

484
00:23:55,267 --> 00:23:56,667
Does that go into
your class fund?

485
00:23:56,667 --> 00:23:59,067
Yes, and then we'll buy
an electric fan with that.

486
00:23:59,067 --> 00:24:00,467
- [LAUGHING]
- [SILLY SFX PLAYING]

487
00:24:00,467 --> 00:24:02,433
- For the classroom?
- Yes, for the classroom.

488
00:24:02,567 --> 00:24:05,767
Did you get awards when
you were still studying?

489
00:24:05,767 --> 00:24:07,767
The only thing I receive
from teachers is an earful,

490
00:24:07,767 --> 00:24:09,067
same with my mother.

491
00:24:09,067 --> 00:24:14,167
It's just that I had a lot
of friends even as a kid, Meme.

492
00:24:14,167 --> 00:24:16,633
- Where did you grow up?
- In a farmland.

493
00:24:16,967 --> 00:24:18,367
- Seriously.
- In Pampanga?

494
00:24:18,367 --> 00:24:20,167
There were fields
behind our house.

495
00:24:20,167 --> 00:24:22,167
So the games I played were
what boys played,

496
00:24:22,167 --> 00:24:23,667
like <i> tumbang preso
</i>(can-knockdown).

497
00:24:23,667 --> 00:24:25,467
So you enjoyed
playing street games?

498
00:24:25,467 --> 00:24:28,233
- Yes.
- You weren't a fan of studying?

499
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:29,633
No, I wasn't.

500
00:24:29,667 --> 00:24:30,867
I was the eldest child.

501
00:24:30,867 --> 00:24:34,367
My routine was attend school,
play games, and go home.

502
00:24:34,367 --> 00:24:38,467
That routine was
all that I had in my mind.

503
00:24:38,467 --> 00:24:40,667
But I didn't get
failing grades because

504
00:24:40,667 --> 00:24:43,067
I sucked up to my teachers.

505
00:24:43,067 --> 00:24:44,567
- [LAUGHTER]
- No, I'm just kidding!

506
00:24:44,567 --> 00:24:46,067
I still reviewed somehow.

507
00:24:46,067 --> 00:24:47,767
Which subject did you excel at?

508
00:24:47,767 --> 00:24:49,267
Mathematics and English.

509
00:24:49,267 --> 00:24:51,067
- Wow!
- Wow!

510
00:24:51,067 --> 00:24:53,533
Don't test me right now!

511
00:24:53,767 --> 00:24:59,167
So, she excels in Mathematics
and English. Okay.

512
00:24:59,167 --> 00:25:00,133
So, you often cut classes.

513
00:25:00,133 --> 00:25:03,133
Did you ever get caught
by your parents?

514
00:25:03,167 --> 00:25:05,633
- I have a story about that.
- Oh?

515
00:25:05,767 --> 00:25:07,767
I got influenced
by my friends, right?

516
00:25:07,767 --> 00:25:08,967
- Oh my god!
- I'm serious!

517
00:25:08,967 --> 00:25:10,167
And then, what happened?

518
00:25:10,167 --> 00:25:13,633
So, I always hanged out
with my friends back then.

519
00:25:13,767 --> 00:25:16,167
I didn't go to school anymore.

520
00:25:16,167 --> 00:25:17,633
- And then...
- How old were you then?

521
00:25:17,767 --> 00:25:19,967
- Sophomore year in high school.
- So, how old were you then?

522
00:25:19,967 --> 00:25:22,967
- About 15?
- Yes, around 14 or 15.

523
00:25:22,967 --> 00:25:24,667
So, I was cutting classes.

524
00:25:24,667 --> 00:25:27,667
My mom found out because when
she received my report card,

525
00:25:27,667 --> 00:25:29,067
my grades were just left blank!

526
00:25:29,067 --> 00:25:32,267
My mom punched me hard
and I turned over on the swing!

527
00:25:32,267 --> 00:25:34,233
This is a true story!
She really punched me.

528
00:25:34,233 --> 00:25:36,733
- You were on a swing?
- Yes, and she punched me!

529
00:25:36,767 --> 00:25:38,967
- "You have no grades, huh?!"
- [LAUGHTER]

530
00:25:38,967 --> 00:25:40,467
Seriously, my mother punched me.

531
00:25:40,467 --> 00:25:42,967
But of course,
I acted innocently.

532
00:25:42,967 --> 00:25:44,767
I didn't do anything wrong!
I always attended my classes!

533
00:25:44,767 --> 00:25:47,267
So, during
the parent-teacher conference,

534
00:25:47,267 --> 00:25:49,033
my teacher explained everything.

535
00:25:49,267 --> 00:25:51,267
Of course, I had
to lie low a little bit.

536
00:25:51,267 --> 00:25:55,067
So for the next quarter,
my grades hit 89 and 90.

537
00:25:55,067 --> 00:25:57,533
- [AUDIENCE EXCLAIMS]
- This is real, promise!

538
00:25:57,767 --> 00:25:59,767
My previously blank grades
were also filled because

539
00:25:59,767 --> 00:26:01,967
my teachers saw that I changed.

540
00:26:01,967 --> 00:26:02,967
[VICE] Ooh.

541
00:26:02,967 --> 00:26:04,967
- And then that continued on?
- No.

542
00:26:04,967 --> 00:26:07,433
- [LAUGHTER]
- [SILLY SFX PLAYING]

543
00:26:07,433 --> 00:26:09,833
I just made up to my mother
a little bit.

544
00:26:09,867 --> 00:26:11,333
- It was an act.
- Honest!

545
00:26:11,567 --> 00:26:13,567
She just waited
to get passing scores.

546
00:26:13,567 --> 00:26:18,567
On my junior year,
I still cut classes a little.

547
00:26:18,567 --> 00:26:20,767
But there were more days
when I attended classes.

548
00:26:20,767 --> 00:26:23,733
By my senior year,
I studied hard already.

549
00:26:23,867 --> 00:26:25,067
Did you graduate
from high school?

550
00:26:25,067 --> 00:26:26,067
I did.

551
00:26:26,067 --> 00:26:27,267
Did you attend college?

552
00:26:27,267 --> 00:26:30,267
I studied for two years
but I eventually dropped out.

553
00:26:30,267 --> 00:26:31,767
[VICE] What program
did you take?

554
00:26:31,767 --> 00:26:34,567
- BS in Tourism Management.
- Yes!

555
00:26:34,567 --> 00:26:36,767
Okay, next.
Who could it be?

556
00:26:36,767 --> 00:26:40,433
Who was a school vendor?

557
00:26:40,433 --> 00:26:44,433
[UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING]

558
00:26:44,467 --> 00:26:51,433
[CHEERING]

559
00:26:51,967 --> 00:26:53,967
You were vendors?

560
00:26:53,967 --> 00:26:56,167
- Yes, Meme.
- What did you sell, Jelai?

561
00:26:56,167 --> 00:27:01,367
I sold corn dogs and meals
with fried rice and egg.

562
00:27:01,367 --> 00:27:04,167
Was your stall outside
or inside the school?

563
00:27:04,167 --> 00:27:05,667
I sold inside our room, Meme.

564
00:27:05,667 --> 00:27:09,967
I lived really close to school.

565
00:27:09,967 --> 00:27:12,167
Did you cook the meals
you sold in your home?

566
00:27:12,167 --> 00:27:13,733
- Yes.
- Or did you buy it cooked?

567
00:27:13,733 --> 00:27:15,933
My mom and I cooked them all.

568
00:27:15,967 --> 00:27:17,967
How much did you sell a <i> tapa
</i>meal with fried rice and egg?

569
00:27:17,967 --> 00:27:19,167
How much was that, Ma?

570
00:27:19,167 --> 00:27:20,367
[VICE] You're the vendor, right?

571
00:27:20,367 --> 00:27:21,767
- [LAUGH TRACK]
- [JELAI CHUCKLES]

572
00:27:21,767 --> 00:27:23,967
Mm, around 80 pesos.

573
00:27:23,967 --> 00:27:28,467
It's a student-friendly price.

574
00:27:28,467 --> 00:27:29,967
It's affordable.

575
00:27:29,967 --> 00:27:32,667
What about you, Juby?
What stuff did you sell?

576
00:27:32,667 --> 00:27:34,367
We sold plenty of stuff, Meme.

577
00:27:34,367 --> 00:27:37,667
Boiled eggs, pork rinds,
junk food,

578
00:27:37,667 --> 00:27:40,267
and juice drinks that
you poke open with straws.

579
00:27:40,267 --> 00:27:42,767
I even carried a cooler.

580
00:27:42,767 --> 00:27:45,067
- Where did you study?
- In Cebu.

581
00:27:45,067 --> 00:27:46,267
Were you already in college
when this started?

582
00:27:46,267 --> 00:27:48,033
Senior high and college.

583
00:27:48,033 --> 00:27:49,933
- Did you sell eggs?
- Yes.

584
00:27:49,967 --> 00:27:51,167
For how much?

585
00:27:51,167 --> 00:27:56,167
In the market,
it only costs 4.50 pesos.

586
00:27:56,167 --> 00:27:58,167
I sold it for seven pesos.

587
00:27:58,167 --> 00:28:01,767
Now when I go to school,
I'd tell my classmates

588
00:28:01,767 --> 00:28:05,167
to sell them for 10 pesos,
I'd get the seven pesos,

589
00:28:05,167 --> 00:28:06,200
and the change will be theirs.

590
00:28:06,200 --> 00:28:09,367
Your boiled eggs cost 10 pesos?
That's expensive!

591
00:28:09,367 --> 00:28:10,867
[CHUCKLES] Because, Meme--

592
00:28:10,867 --> 00:28:14,067
How much salt do you put
in it for it to cost 10 pesos?

593
00:28:14,067 --> 00:28:16,067
She used an ostrich egg.

594
00:28:16,067 --> 00:28:17,767
She uses mineral water
to boil them.

595
00:28:17,767 --> 00:28:18,800
Right.

596
00:28:18,900 --> 00:28:20,533
So what's the reason why
you became a school vendor?

597
00:28:20,533 --> 00:28:23,033
I had to. I didn't have money
to pay fare.

598
00:28:23,067 --> 00:28:24,233
Gosh!

599
00:28:24,467 --> 00:28:26,933
- Oh!
- She's about to cry.

600
00:28:27,367 --> 00:28:29,167
My makeup's too good
for me to cry in it.

601
00:28:29,167 --> 00:28:30,967
[LAUGHTER]

602
00:28:30,967 --> 00:28:33,767
I couldn't afford to pay fare
and buy books.

603
00:28:33,767 --> 00:28:35,767
- I can't cry this early!
- [LAUGHTER]

604
00:28:35,767 --> 00:28:37,367
So, is there a specific time
for you to cry?

605
00:28:37,367 --> 00:28:38,867
- Yes!
- She set her mind to it.

606
00:28:38,867 --> 00:28:41,667
She has to cry before
the program ends.

607
00:28:41,667 --> 00:28:44,633
She can't cry early.
It has to be near the end.

608
00:28:45,067 --> 00:28:47,667
Oh, so it was a necessity.

609
00:28:47,667 --> 00:28:50,867
Yes. I also had to buy rice
and that stuff.

610
00:28:50,867 --> 00:28:52,033
[INHALES SHARPLY]

611
00:28:53,167 --> 00:28:54,633
[CRYING]

612
00:28:54,633 --> 00:28:55,833
[EXCLAIMS]

613
00:28:55,867 --> 00:28:56,867
[SOMBER MUSIC PLAYING]

614
00:28:56,867 --> 00:28:58,367
I'm just curious.

615
00:28:58,367 --> 00:29:01,567
Why do we play sad music when
someone tells a sad story?

616
00:29:01,567 --> 00:29:04,967
- [LAUGHTER]
- [SILLY SFX PLAYING]

617
00:29:04,967 --> 00:29:07,767
Why are we making
the person feel lonelier?

618
00:29:07,767 --> 00:29:10,567
It's as if we're pushing
someone to cry

619
00:29:10,567 --> 00:29:13,567
because emotional scenes
are good for the ratings.

620
00:29:13,567 --> 00:29:15,167
[TETAY] We're egging her
on to cry.

621
00:29:15,167 --> 00:29:16,167
[VICE] Right.

622
00:29:16,167 --> 00:29:20,167
How were you growing up?

623
00:29:20,167 --> 00:29:22,633
How was your situation
in life at the time?

624
00:29:23,367 --> 00:29:24,533
Um...

625
00:29:25,467 --> 00:29:27,433
[CHUCKLES] I was about
to say that since I was four...

626
00:29:27,433 --> 00:29:29,933
You had no money and you
still dyed your hair that way.

627
00:29:29,967 --> 00:29:31,367
- [LAUGHTER]
- [SILLY SFX PLAYING]

628
00:29:31,367 --> 00:29:32,867
Our neighbors are
also like that.

629
00:29:32,867 --> 00:29:34,367
They may be drowning in debt
and they'll always have no money

630
00:29:34,367 --> 00:29:35,967
but they will always
have their hair done.

631
00:29:35,967 --> 00:29:39,967
They even have makeup on
their eyebrows and lips, too.

632
00:29:39,967 --> 00:29:43,133
So, please tell us about
your situation back then.

633
00:29:43,267 --> 00:29:44,767
Well, um...

634
00:29:44,767 --> 00:29:47,567
When we were here in Manila,
we lived comfortably.

635
00:29:47,567 --> 00:29:50,567
But when the company
my father worked at closed,

636
00:29:50,567 --> 00:29:54,567
we had to move to Cebu.

637
00:29:54,567 --> 00:29:57,767
When we got to Cebu,
we really had to start over.

638
00:29:57,767 --> 00:30:01,200
So, we just rented
a room that was...

639
00:30:01,733 --> 00:30:03,533
just this small.
It was really tiny, Meme.

640
00:30:03,567 --> 00:30:07,367
Our living room, kitchen,
and bedroom was all in one room.

641
00:30:07,367 --> 00:30:11,867
My siblings and I were
all still studying, too.

642
00:30:11,867 --> 00:30:14,867
As for my mother's work,

643
00:30:14,867 --> 00:30:17,367
it was a stay-in job
but the pay wasn't enough

644
00:30:17,367 --> 00:30:20,167
because we had bills for
rent, water, and everything.

645
00:30:20,167 --> 00:30:22,667
We also had to buy
new appliances

646
00:30:22,667 --> 00:30:25,133
because we were just
renting the place.

647
00:30:25,767 --> 00:30:27,967
And so, I was like...

648
00:30:27,967 --> 00:30:29,767
I was embarrassed of myself

649
00:30:29,767 --> 00:30:31,867
because my siblings would work
as jeepney dispatchers

650
00:30:31,867 --> 00:30:34,333
and sell <i> otap</i>   in the piers.

651
00:30:34,333 --> 00:30:37,533
I also felt embarrassed to ask
money for fare every day.

652
00:30:37,567 --> 00:30:40,867
Sometimes, they would already
scratch early in the morning

653
00:30:40,867 --> 00:30:41,867
so I couldn't ask anyone
for money.

654
00:30:41,867 --> 00:30:43,267
So, I thought to...

655
00:30:43,267 --> 00:30:44,767
- Scratch? Or dispatch?
- Oh!

656
00:30:44,767 --> 00:30:47,267
- Sorry, dispatch!
- They were scratching.

657
00:30:47,267 --> 00:30:50,067
- Scratching.
- [JUBY LAUGHING]

658
00:30:50,067 --> 00:30:53,267
My tears went back in.

659
00:30:53,267 --> 00:30:55,667
And, um...

660
00:30:55,667 --> 00:31:01,167
I thought that if someone paid
me to do their project,

661
00:31:01,167 --> 00:31:02,367
I would save that money

662
00:31:02,367 --> 00:31:04,967
and use that money as capital
to start a small business.

663
00:31:04,967 --> 00:31:06,733
- You did others' projects?
- Yes.

664
00:31:06,733 --> 00:31:09,233
So, how did you do it?
You said your home was small.

665
00:31:09,267 --> 00:31:11,467
The kitchen and the bedroom
was the same, right?

666
00:31:11,467 --> 00:31:13,167
- The dining room, too.
- Yes.

667
00:31:13,167 --> 00:31:15,933
How do you eat if
someone's still sleeping?

668
00:31:16,167 --> 00:31:18,167
They have no choice.
We'll wake them up.

669
00:31:18,167 --> 00:31:21,667
You'll put their plate of food
above them as they sleep.

670
00:31:21,667 --> 00:31:24,467
Yes, sometimes...

671
00:31:24,467 --> 00:31:25,967
[AUDIENCE GUSHES]

672
00:31:25,967 --> 00:31:29,467
Hey! Jo, sorry.
It's just for work.

673
00:31:29,467 --> 00:31:30,967
Give me that. I'll also cry!

674
00:31:30,967 --> 00:31:35,467
- [LAUGHTER]
- [LAUGH TRACK]

675
00:31:35,467 --> 00:31:37,267
Don't worry. If you cry,
Verna will comfort you.

676
00:31:37,267 --> 00:31:39,067
It's fine. I love you, Verna.

677
00:31:39,067 --> 00:31:40,433
Okay.

678
00:31:40,433 --> 00:31:41,933
It's really nice to hear.

679
00:31:41,933 --> 00:31:46,933
From that situation,
you're now here, right?

680
00:31:46,967 --> 00:31:50,167
Wow, you're better off now.

681
00:31:50,167 --> 00:31:51,267
Yes.

682
00:31:51,267 --> 00:31:56,767
I could now send money
to treat my family, like pizza.

683
00:31:56,767 --> 00:31:58,967
Back then, I really couldn't
afford that.

684
00:31:58,967 --> 00:32:01,167
You're sending a whole pizza
to them from Manila to Cebu?

685
00:32:01,167 --> 00:32:03,167
You can do that!

686
00:32:03,167 --> 00:32:04,867
People can do that!

687
00:32:04,867 --> 00:32:07,367
You just have to adjust
the location and

688
00:32:07,367 --> 00:32:09,833
the settings of
the app you are using.

689
00:32:10,067 --> 00:32:11,267
From Manila,
you'll deliver a...

690
00:32:11,267 --> 00:32:13,833
No, not from Manila!
The pizza's from Cebu.

691
00:32:13,833 --> 00:32:15,633
I'll just set my location there.

692
00:32:15,667 --> 00:32:17,467
But can you say that
you feel more loose now?

693
00:32:17,467 --> 00:32:19,267
- Yes, because...
- Are you really loose now?

694
00:32:19,267 --> 00:32:21,767
- Oh, no! What "loose?"
- [LAUGHTER]

695
00:32:21,767 --> 00:32:24,167
I meant like your pockets
aren't as tight now.

696
00:32:24,167 --> 00:32:25,300
[LAUGHS]

697
00:32:25,300 --> 00:32:27,967
There are times when we feel
like the world's so wide,

698
00:32:27,967 --> 00:32:29,967
but sometimes,
it feels so narrow.

699
00:32:29,967 --> 00:32:32,567
How about you?
Is it wide or narrow?

700
00:32:32,567 --> 00:32:34,067
- [LAUGHTER]
- [JUBY GIGGLES]

701
00:32:34,067 --> 00:32:36,867
Because when you're getting
more popular,

702
00:32:36,867 --> 00:32:39,367
there's an irony in there.

703
00:32:39,367 --> 00:32:44,133
When you get famous,
your world feels so small.

704
00:32:44,767 --> 00:32:45,767
Right?

705
00:32:45,767 --> 00:32:47,233
That's why I asked
that question.

706
00:32:47,233 --> 00:32:49,633
Is yours wide or narrow?

707
00:32:49,667 --> 00:32:52,367
It's 50/50. It's a bit wide
and a bit narrow.

708
00:32:52,367 --> 00:32:53,867
[LAUGHTER]

709
00:32:53,867 --> 00:32:55,167
What's wide?

710
00:32:55,167 --> 00:32:57,367
- Life is.
- What's narrow?

711
00:32:57,367 --> 00:33:01,967
It's the world that we move in
as Baby Dolls.

712
00:33:01,967 --> 00:33:03,133
[VICE] Ah, okay.

713
00:33:03,267 --> 00:33:04,467
Okay, next.

714
00:33:04,467 --> 00:33:06,667
[LAUGHTER]

715
00:33:06,667 --> 00:33:12,867
Who among you has that beauty
that people fight over?

716
00:33:12,867 --> 00:33:19,833
[UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING]

717
00:33:22,967 --> 00:33:24,167
[JOHAIRA] I need to be there.

718
00:33:24,167 --> 00:33:26,467
She's disagreeing. She's saying
she also has to be included.

719
00:33:26,467 --> 00:33:28,667
- [SILLY SFX PLAYING]
- Go, go!

720
00:33:28,667 --> 00:33:32,833
Hey! Hey! Hey! Hey!
Hey! Hey! Hey!

721
00:33:33,467 --> 00:33:35,967
Yes, I think I also have
to join in.

722
00:33:35,967 --> 00:33:38,967
- Yes!
- Just kidding!

723
00:33:38,967 --> 00:33:40,133
[TETAY] You should!

724
00:33:40,667 --> 00:33:43,867
What do you mean by beauty
that people fight over?

725
00:33:43,867 --> 00:33:45,333
Should I go first?

726
00:33:45,567 --> 00:33:51,067
Well, I was always sent to
